  "articleBody": "Upgrading an attached or detached garage often involves common finish carpentry blunders that leave the room looking sloppy and vulnerable to wear. Avoiding these pitfalls keeps your space durable and attractive.\n\n1. Skipping material acclimation: Installing trim right off the delivery truck leads to open miter joints and buckled boards once the lumber adjusts to garage humidity. Allow stock to stabilize to dry ambient conditions first.\n\n2. Using interior MDF near wet floor zones: Standard MDF absorbs moisture readily. Running MDF baseboards against concrete slabs or near vehicle parking spots invites swelling, peeling paint, and mold. Choose moisture-resistant solid wood or composite profiles instead.\n\n3. Cutting identical lengths based on nominal plans: Garage walls and floors rarely form true ninety-degree angles. Measuring each piece at its exact wall position prevents unsightly gaps and crooked joints.\n\n4. Fastening trim into sheetrock alone: Securing casing or baseboards only to drywall paper rather than solid wall studs causes boards to pull loose over time, especially around heavy access doors.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps:\nHandling complex wall transitions, out-of-square corners, or built-in work calls for experienced hands. Abraham Becerra and the team at HH Remodeling LLC bring years of dedicated finish carpentry and remodeling experience to homeowners across Denver, Centennial, and Lakewood. Call 303-505-0866 to plan your garage finish carpentry project today.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
